  "query_preview": "(Today through Thursday)...\nIssued at 403 AM EDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nToday through Saturday night...\n\nHigh humidity and seasonably warm conditions will continue through \nthis weekend across Indiana amid at times southwesterly breezes and \noverall higher heights from stronger upper trough retracted to Upper \nMidwest and north.  A few weak ingredients for convection should \ncome together at times, especially through early evening and perhaps \nagain for several hours Saturday, although confidence is low at any \ngiven time for more than widely scattered showers and/or more than \nisolated non-impactful storms.  Rain chances to trend from west to \neast today through early evening, with lower certainty for this \nmorning's POPs and higher confidence in at least briefly scattered \ncoverage from this afternoon along/south of I-70 and especially \nlater today over eastern counties.  Greatest potential impacts will \ncontinue to be ponding of low-lying areas from localized downpours.\n\nPotential for more scattered convection around the midday to early \nevening timeframe on Saturday should small wave coming from northern \nIllinois arrive late enough to catch daytime heating, which is not \nexpected.  More likely scenario is isolated showers early Saturday \nand perhaps a few organized RW/TRW- after 18Z south/east of \nIndianapolis. Only foreseeable impact would again be high water \nfollowing any downpours.\n\nSunday through Thursday...\n\nFrom a synoptic standpoint, next week will be characterized by quasi-\nzonal or very weakly cyclonic flow, with broad subtropical ridging \nstretching from the southeastern CONUS into the Four Corners region, \nevolving into a more amplified pattern as the ridge near and just \neast of the west coast, with stronger upper level troughing dropping \ninto the upper Midwest and Great Lakes regions late week.\n\nThis will translate into near normal temperatures early in the week \ncooling slightly to just below normal by the latter part of the work \nweek. Additionally, low to moderate precipitation chances early in \nthe week will accompany low amplitude, low predictability shortwave \ndisturbances, with somewhat higher rain chances late in the week as \nlarger scale troughing enters the picture.\n\nDespite the more limited rain chances early and higher chances late \nin the week, heavy rainfall will remain a primary concern throughout \nthe week, as GEFS Integrated Water Vapor Transport progs show robust \nmoisture advection into or near the region much of the week, with \nregional precipitable water values near to well above climatological \nmean, and seasonably high wet bulb zero heights on the order of 12-\n14kft much of the week all pointing toward high rainfall efficiency \nin a background setting of plentiful recent rains.\n\nExperimental machine learning guidance suggests at least a low \nchance for a few strong to severe storms at times, but \npredictability limits inherent in the overall synoptic pattern along \nwith those accompanying convection that is largely \nthermodynamically/cold pool driven limit the ability to focus in \nmore detail on a particular period for severe risk, aside from \nstating the obvious that late afternoon to evening time frames would \nlikely be the primary concern for most days at least until late in \nthe week when more significant synoptic forcing becomes a factor.",
